# SeriesX Emulator (experimental)

Status: **v0.15** (engine version 0.15.0, experimental; release candidate for 1.0.0-experimental). SeriesX has a graphical launcher and game library, an executable **loader/inspector**, a guest process/thread/synchronisation layer,
an x86-64 interpreter (integer ISA, SSE/SSE2, AVX/AVX2, FMA3, BMI - validated against real hardware), a small **Win32 test layer** and, new in v0.6, a first **Xbox runtime layer**
that serves a handful of publicly documented `XGameRuntime` functions (initialisation and task queues) to guest programs. The programs that run end-to-end today are test programs
**we build ourselves**. **The engine cannot run any commercial game**; it does not emulate Xbox hardware, the Xbox kernel, the GPU or any Xbox service, and no compatibility with any
commercial title is claimed or tested. Research notes (verified facts / experimental findings / unknowns, with sources): `docs/XBOX_RESEARCH.md`; design: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`.

## External rendering experiment (25 November 2026)

The standalone DexNom fork of VIctory reached the GTA VI opening cutscene in Series X mode on Windows with a Ryzen 5 5600X, Radeon RX 6600 (8 GB) and 16 GB of RAM. On a second launch with the shader and pipeline cache from the first run, the default renderer showed terrain, trees, characters and several camera shots, including a waterfront at dawn. Shadows remained very dark and the world often ran below 1 FPS. Player control remains unverified.

This experiment used DexNom's standalone launcher and measures that runtime's compatibility. See the [tested DexNom revision](https://github.com/DexNom/VIctory/commit/921d8d3b43b44f3a6ca4b44095edd3c4baf10a87) and the [test results](https://github.com/anrq0/SeriesX-Emulator/issues/1#issuecomment-7168908492).

## Game Sources (games stay where they are)
Add the folders where your games already are (**Game Sources** page or `config/game_sources.json` in the data folder). SeriesX reads them in place, never copies or
modifies them, keeps only metadata and indexes, resumes interrupted scans, finds duplicates and tells you exactly what a title is missing - for an Xbox title without
executable code that is *your own plain game files* (encrypted packages are never opened). A **download manager** fetches approved, commit-pinned test material from official
sources and a **test lab** runs ranked test programs through the engines (`seriesx --lab`). See [docs/architecture/GAME_SOURCES.md](docs/architecture/GAME_SOURCES.md)
and [docs/compatibility/COMMERCIAL_GAME_TESTING.md](docs/compatibility/COMMERCIAL_GAME_TESTING.md). **No commercial Xbox title has been tested.**

## Execution engines (multi-engine, experimental)
Press **Play** and the Game Compatibility Manager picks the engine that can attempt the title, or explains exactly what is missing:
the SeriesX interpreter, a native Windows launch (Windows PC programs only), or **ScarlettX** run as a separate process when you have built it
locally (`pwsh tools/fetch_upstream.ps1` then `pwsh tools/build_upstream.ps1`). Native engines are disabled until you allow native execution
in Settings > Emulator. **No commercial Xbox Series X|S title is known to run.** See [docs/engines/ENGINE_COMPARISON.md](docs/engines/ENGINE_COMPARISON.md),
[docs/KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md](docs/KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md) and the **Performance** page for measured metrics.

## Playing a program

SeriesX can execute an **internal test program live**: open the Game Library, click *SeriesX Breakout*, press **Play**, and a real game runs on the interpreter in the *Now Playing* page (picture, keyboard/controller input, measured fps and instruction counts, a STOP button). It is an original program written for SeriesX with its own display ABI - **not an Xbox title** and not Xbox-compatible; no commercial Xbox game runs. Details, screenshots and limits: [docs/PLAYING.md](docs/PLAYING.md); why no Xbox-targeted path exists yet: [docs/XBOX_EXECUTION_PATH.md](docs/XBOX_EXECUTION_PATH.md).

